    "q-22787": {
      "dom_id": "step-q22787",
      "name": "Question 3",
      "problem": "What is the slope of the line $2x+5y=1?$",
      "worked_solution": "We can find the slope of the line if we can express its equation in slope-intercept form. To do this, we solve for $y,$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2x+5y & =1 \\\\ 5y & =-2x+1 \\\\ \\frac{5y}{5} & =\\frac{-2x+1}{5} \\\\ y & =-\\frac{2x}{5}+\\frac{1}{5} \\\\ y & =-\\frac{2}{5}x+\\frac{1}{5}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFrom the slope-intercept equation, we can tell that the slope of the line is $m=-\\frac{2}{5}.$",
      "calculator_instructions": "",
