      "name": "Question 6",
      "problem": "The line $3x-14y=-3$ intersects the $x$-axis at the point$\\,($\n{{field-1}}\n$,$\n{{field-2}}\n$).$",
      "worked_solution": "To find the $x$-intercept, we substitute $y=0$ into the equation and solve for $x.$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}3x-14y & =-3 \\\\ 3x-14(0) & =-3 \\\\ 3x & =-3 \\\\ \\frac{3x}{3} & =\\frac{-3}{3} \\\\ x & =-1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHence, the $x$-intercept is $x=-1.$ Thus, the line intersects the $x$-axis at $(-1,0).$",
