    "q-214680": {
      "dom_id": "step-q214680",
      "name": "Question 7",
      "problem": "The line $3x+8y=18$ intersects the $x$-axis at the point$\\,($\n{{field-1}}\n$,$\n{{field-2}}\n$).$",
      "worked_solution": "To find the $x$-intercept, we substitute $y=0$ into the equation and solve for $x.$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}3x+8y & =18 \\\\ 3x+8(0) & =18 \\\\ 3x & =18 \\\\ \\frac{3x}{3} & =\\frac{18}{3} \\\\ x & =6\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHence, the $x$-intercept is $x=6.$ Thus, the line intersects the $x$-axis at $(6,0).$",
